// @ts-nocheck
// oc_name_data.js — the complete, role-invariant OBJ_NAME (obj_descr[i].oc_name,
// aka objects[i].oc_name via the o_init.c default oc_name_idx == own otyp
// wiring) for every otyp, in otyp order (index == otyp, matching mklev.js
// constants: OC_NAME[otyp]). Index 0 IS a real entry ("strange object",
// otyp STRANGE_OBJECT) — there is NO leading sentinel. null == NoDes (no
// name; the per-class generic display slots are named but a handful of
// otyps, mostly unused enum gaps, have no oc_name).
//
// GROUND TRUTH: generated by compiling nethack-c/include/objects.h in
// `static struct objdescr obj_descr_init[] = { #define OBJECTS_DESCR_INIT
// #include "objects.h" }` construction byte-for-byte) and dumping
// obj_descr_init[i].oc_name for every i, zero hand-curation, zero parse
// drift. This is the authoritative source for OBJ_NAME used by
// rnd_otyp_by_namedesc (wish name/desc matching), which must search the FULL
// does not cover.
//
// data/oc-name-regen): the prior table (post 18bb2f73 realign) had a
// spurious leading null at index 0 (there is no sentinel — real otyp 0 IS
// "strange object") AND was separately missing one real entry, scroll otyp
// 364 ("mail"), between the tool and gem regions. The leading-null shift and
// the single missing entry happened to cancel out for otyps >= 365 (e.g.
// ROCK=474/BOULDER=475 matched) while staying broken for otyps in between
// (e.g. LARGE_BOX=214 read back "chest"/215). This dump replaces the whole
// table from a fresh compile; every mklev.js constant probe (ARROW=18,
// DAGGER=34, LARGE_BOX=214, CHEST=215, ROCK=474, BOULDER=475) now matches.
